r sees the other'sarticles.  UUCPbb files an article as SPOOL/news/COMP/OS/OS9/a1, readingits group list from SYS/UUCP/active; MNews files it asSPOOL/MNEWS/comp/os/os9/1, with its own active file in USR/LIB/NEWS.MNews was written for /h0/SPOOL/NEWS.  RBF names do not tell case apart,so on a disk that also carries UUCPbb that is the SAME directory asSPOOL/news -- which is why MNews here was built to use SPOOL/MNEWSinstead.  SRC/mnews/README.OSK lists that change.  C News was moved toSPOOL/CNEWS for the same reason (SRC/cnews/README.OSK).UUCPbb's readnews and rn keep your place in the SAME FILE, .newsrc in yourhome directory, in the same `group: numbers' form -- but each countsarticles by its own spool's numbering.  Run both from one home directoryand each reads the other's numbers as its own.  nnkeeps its own, .nn/rc.UUCPbb and MNews do share one layer: both ride on UUCP to reach anothersite.  MNews's sbatch hands its batches to `uux', and its rnews mailsthrough CMDS/UUCP/rmail, both from the UUCP set in CMDS/UUCP.UUCPbb's news -- the small set beside UUCPbb's mail---------------------------------------------------  Part of UUCPbb 2.1, Bob Billson's UUCP for OS-9, GPL v2.  rnews and  expire are from its patch 1.  The manuals are in DOC/uucpbb, already  formatted: readnews.man, postnews.man, rnews.man, subscribe.man.    rnews        takes a batch apart and files each article; a compressed                 batch it hands to `compress'    readnews     reads news a prompt at a time: `y', `n', `q', and `h' at                 any prompt for the commands.  New groups in the active                 file are offered one by one.  .newsrc in your home                 directory keeps your place    postnews     asks for the header fields, then posts the article                 locally and queues it for your UUCP neighbour.  The                 Distribution must be one listed in                 SYS/UUCP/distributions    expire       deletes articles past their date    subscribe, unsubscribe                 turn a group on or off in .newsrc  WHAT IT NEEDS: TERM set -- readnews stops with "Unknown terminal type"  without one -- and SPOOL/news in place before rnews or readnews will  start.  postnews forks `rnews' by name, so UUCP/rnews has to be  resident; without it, measured:      docmd_na(): cannot spawn process--> rnews -x0 -ncomp.os.os9 ...  With it loaded, the same posting lands as SPOOL/news/COMP/OS/OS9/a1.  Costs: no full-screen reader, and all six need cio.  `unsubscribe' on a  group that is already off prints a number where the group's name should  be -- a `%' in its message with no conversion letter after it.  To leave readnews: `q'.C News and rn -- the Unix pair------------------------------  C News is Geoff Collyer and Henry Spencer's news transport, University  of Toronto, 1985-1990: everything in a news system except the reader.  blarson's OS-9 port arrived in an archive named GNU_ATP_1_40.lzh, which  is why that name turns up in its provenance.  Terms: use and  redistribution by anyone, with four conditions about credit --  DOC/cnews/COPYRIGHT.  rn 4.3 is Larry Wall's reader, Bob Larson's port:  copy it in whole or in part, but do not try to make money off it or  pretend that you wrote it.    relaynews    files an article or a batch under its newsgroup, numbers                 it, updates the active file and the history, and refuses                 a Message-ID it has already seen    newsrun      feeds relaynews everything waiting in in.coming, with                 trlf to give a Unix batch OS-9's line endings    rn           the reader: `rn <group>', y reads, space pages, q leaves    Pnews        posts: it asks for the distribution, newsgroup and                 subject, or `Pnews -h <file> -s' sends a file that has                 its header    newsetup     writes a .newsrc from the active file, every group                 marked unsubscribed until you choose    bdecode, c7decode   undo the encodings a batch is put into to cross a                        link that loses the eighth bit    dbz, newshist       build the history index, and look a message-id                        up in it    byteflip, newslock  reorder a dbz index made on another machine; take                        the news system's lock by hand  Configuration is USR/LIB/CNEWS -- active, sys, history with its dbz  index, artnum -- and rn's library is USR/LIB/RN.  HOW AN ARTICLE GOES ROUND, measured 2026-09-23:      Pnews -h post -s              leaves it as SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local1      newsrun                       files it as SPOOL/CNEWS/junk/1, and                                    removes it from in.coming      rn junk                       offers `1 unread article in junk'  newsrun takes everything waiting in in.coming, a post or a batch from  another site, compressed or not, with Unix line endings or OS-9's.  It  runs `trlf' -- written for this disk, since blarson's is in no archive --  and `compress' through your own OS-9's `shell', so `load  /dd/CMDS/NEWS/trlf' first.  Measured 2026-09-23.  relaynews by hand,  `relaynews -r -n < SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local1', does the same for one  file and leaves it in in.coming.  WHAT rn NEEDS:  1. A RAM disk at /r0, where it keeps its scratch files.  On a real     system that is your own /r0; under os9exec, `mount -r=256k /r0'.     Without one rn stops with `Can't open /r0/rnvary.3' -- and says     `.newsrc restored' while leaving it renamed to .oldnewsrc.  Rename     it back before trying again.  2. A .newsrc.  With none, rn runs newsetup through your own OS-9's     `shell' -- SYS/login loads it from /h1 under os9exec.  Without a     shell it answers `Can't create a .newsrc--you must do it yourself';     running `newsetup' yourself does it, and needs nothing else.  3. A News directory under HOME to save articles in.  Without one rn     says `Cannot make directory .../News' and saves them in HOME.  Costs: newsrun wants trlf loaded, and rn's `h' help pages  were shell scripts for a Unix system and are not here -- DOC/rn's manual  page is the reference.  The six older tools need cio.  To leave rn: q.MNews and nn -- a news system and a full-screen reader------------------------------------------------------  Ulrich Dessauer's MNews, a news system written for OS-9 (1990-92), with  his port of Kim F. Storm's nn 6.3 newsreader.  All eleven are built here  from SRC/mnews, trap-free.  Dessauer's terms ask that the package travel  WHOLE, so SRC/mnews is the complete package; SOURCES.txt, under `MNEWS  AND NN', has the terms in full.    inews        files an article: `inews -h < article' posts one,                 `inews -c=newgroup:<group>' makes a group    rnews        unpacks a batch from another site and hands each article                 to inews    sbatch       collects articles for a neighbour into batches, and hands                 them to uux    nn           the reader: a full-screen menu of articles by subject,                 threads, kill files, `?' for help    nnmaster     keeps nn's database, SPOOL/NN    nnaux        what nn runs to post, follow up or reply    nncheck      says whether there is news you have not read    nnadmin      looks inside nn's database; `nnadmin C' shows where                 everything is    encode, decode, cvt_help                 B News 2.11's seven-bit coder, and nn's help converter  WHAT IT NEEDS, IN ORDER.  Each of these was measured 2026-09-23:  1. `setup', once each time the system starts, before anything else.     Every MNews program reads TOP Munich's SysInfo table -- host name, spool     directories, locks -- and setup builds it from SYS/sysinfo.  Without     it inews stops at once with `inews: Can't initialize the mess'.  2. Your own OS-9's `shell', for nnmaster.  nnmaster runs commands     through `shell'; on a real OS-9 system that is /dd/CMDS/shell and is     always there.  Under os9exec, SYS/login loads yours from /h1.     Without it `nnmaster -I' prints `Initializing master data base...'     and never returns.  With it, `nnmaster -I' (answer OK, then y to     reuse the GROUPS file) and then `nnmaster' build the database nn     reads.  The GROUPS file -I makes is readable by its owner only     (measured 2026-09-23, running as `tester'): nnmaster runs as the     owner of its module, and the file comes out `------wr'.  Anyone else's     nn and nncheck then stop with `Cannot open file .../GROUPS' -- so as     the super-user follow `nnmaster -I' with         attr -pr /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS  3. inews resident before rnews runs.  rnews forks `inews' by bare name,     and inews lives in CMDS/MNEWS, not in the execution directory.     Without it rnews does not stop: it repeats         Can't execute 'inews'     for as long as you let it.  `load /dd/CMDS/MNEWS/inews' first and     the same batch reports `Received 1 articles'.  4. uux resident before sbatch runs, for the same reason -- otherwise     `Can't fork 'uux ...' (errno = 221)'.  5. For a compressed batch, a module named `uncompress': rnews forks it     by that name.  6. HOME and USER set, and TERM for nn.  nn keeps each reader's place in     .nn under HOME, and its first run makes that directory by running     `makdir' through `shell' -- your own OS-9's, both of them, which     SYS/login loads from /h1 under os9exec.  With neither resident nn     still opens its welcome screen, but makes no .nn, and nncheck goes     on saying `nn has not been invoked to initialize user files'.  Costs: the largest of the three, and several steps before the  first article can be read.  A period bug in inews writes the Date:  header's year as years since 1900 -- an article posted in 2026 says  `Date: 23 Sep 126'.  To leave nn: Q.CHOOSING--------  You want threads, kill files and online help           MNews + nn  You have no cio and cannot get one                     MNews + nn, or                                                         relaynews and rn  You want Larry Wall's rn                               C News + rn  You run UUCPbb for mail and want news beside it        UUCPbb  You want the fewest steps before the first article     UUCPbb  If you take exactly one: MNews with nn -- the whole of CMDS/MNEWS, with  `setup', SYS/sysinfo, USR/LIB/NEWS, USR/LIB/NN and the SPOOL  directories.  Run `setup' first, and load inews before rnews.